the biggest gathering London has seen in  weeks and one of the most passionate  [Music]  protesters were peaceful but angry about  George Floyd's death in the u.s. that  America's woes of inequality and racism  resonated in their lives here  Star Wars starred John boyega  said it was time for change police  chiefs in the UK offered a message of  solidarity to the protesters saying they  too were appalled and horrified by mr.  Floyd's death but that didn't go down  well here in this country we talked and  we're heading for central London police  were issued a warning saying that the  virus is still out there there are still  restrictions in place to prevent its  spread including people live gathering  in groups of more than six people and  staying two metres apart but clearly  that's not happening  people here felt they needed to come  together to make their message loud and  clear there were no mass-produced  placards just simple messages on cargo  and just as police couldn't enforce  social distancing they didn't properly  cordon the route as the March weaved  through traffic on Hyde Park Corner  [Music]  George Floyd died in Minneapolis after a  police officer put a knee on his neck  for eight minutes  it has parallels with Sean Riggs death  in Brixton in 2008 after being held  facedown for several minutes by police  officers  Sean's sister Marcia says families of  those who've died in custody have  recently written asking to meet the  Prime Minister I want people to know  that this has been happening in the  United Kingdom for decades as well  including with my brother Sean and to  see that video on the TV was so  traumatic for me because it just brought  brought my experience back I've shown  it's like it happened yesterday I want  to meet him with Boris Johnson so I  could speak on behalf of all the  families that cannot do it I'm here to  do it for them because I know what it's  like as the protests reached Downey  Street there were some scuffles between  police and protesters  [Music]  but in Brixton there was a more peaceful  moment of reflection farmer were among  those paying tribute to the black lives  matter cause one man's death has  rekindled this movement around the world  and shown that a pandemic can't stop  protests  Jason Farrell Sky News
 

